1. Concept of class metadata in the context of Java class loading?
   a. What is class metadata in Java class loading?
      Class metadata is the information the JVM stores about a loaded class so it can understand and execute that class.
      It includes information such as:
      ->Class name — for example, Employee
      ->Parent class and interfaces — which class it extends and interfaces it implements
      ->Fields — variables declared in the class
      ->Methods — method names, parameters, and return types
      ->Constructors — information about constructors
      ->Runtime information — access modifiers and other information needed by the JVM

2. How does it work during class loading?
   ->Loading: The JVM loads the class bytecode.
   ->Linking: The JVM verifies the bytecode, prepares the class, and resolves symbolic references as needed.
   ->Initialization: The JVM executes static initializers and initializes static variables.
   The JVM maintains class metadata as part of making the class available for execution.

3. What happens when we try to reuse a stream?
   A Java Stream can be consumed only once. Once a terminal operation is performed on a stream, we cannot reuse that stream for
   another operation. If we try to reuse it, Java throws an IllegalStateException because the stream has already been operated
   upon or closed.

   Example:-
   List<Integer> list = List.of(10, 20, 30);
   Stream<Integer> stream = list.stream();
   stream.forEach(System.out::println); // First use
   stream.count(); // Throws IllegalStateException

   How to reuse the data? Create a new stream from the collection.
   list.stream().forEach(System.out::println);
   long count = list.stream().count();
   Note:-
   A stream can have multiple intermediate operations, but it can be consumed by only one terminal operation.

4. How can null be handled in TreeSet?
   By default, TreeSet does not allow null when using natural ordering.
   Interview answer: “We can allow a null value in a TreeSet by using a custom comparator, such as
   Comparator.nullsFirst(Comparator.naturalOrder()). This handles null comparisons and places null first in the sorted set.”

