6. Serial GC
    Your answer: Single-threaded applications and STW.
    Correct. Serial GC uses a single thread for garbage collection and pauses application threads during collection
    (Stop-The-World).
    Suitable for small heaps and small applications.
    Simple, with low GC overhead in suitable workloads.
    Can cause longer pauses as the heap grows.

7. Parallel GC
    Your answer: Multiple threads for GC and high throughput.
    Uses multiple GC threads to collect garbage.
    Focuses on high throughput rather than minimizing pause times.
    Application threads are paused during its stop-the-world collection phases.

8. G1GC
    Your answer: Heap divided into regions, prioritizes regions with more garbage, low latency.
    Divides the heap into multiple regions instead of fixed-size contiguous young and old spaces.
    Prioritizes regions expected to yield the most reclaimable space relative to collection cost.
    Uses evacuation to copy surviving objects out of selected regions.
    Aims for predictable pause times, balancing latency and throughput.

9. ZGC
    Application threads and GC work concurrently.
    Designed for very low pause times.
    Performs much of its work concurrently with application threads.
    Uses techniques such as colored pointers and load barriers in HotSpot implementations.
    Concurrent work still consumes CPU, and pauses are not completely eliminated.

